arm64: canonicalize unsupported PSTATE fields in exception frames

The Harmony ARM identity advertises ID_AA64PFR1_EL1=0 and the guest kernel is
built without CONFIG_ARM64_MTE or CONFIG_ARM64_BTI.  PSTATE.TCO and
PSTATE.BTYPE are therefore outside the guest contract.  A physical KVM CPU
nevertheless supplies both fields on exception entry, while the HVF substrate
leaves them clear.  Nested exceptions copied those substrate bits into struct
pt_regs: otherwise identical M5 RAM images differed only in the corresponding
saved-pstate bits.

Under the cooperative Harmony clock configuration, clear TCO and BTYPE from
SPSR_EL1 before Linux publishes it in an exception frame.  This is an
architectural no-op for the admitted no-MTE/no-BTI guest and prevents an
unsupported host feature from becoming persistent guest RAM or snapshot state.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/entry.S
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/entry.S
@@ -281,6 +281,14 @@ alternative_else_nop_endif
 	.endif /* \el == 0 */
 	mrs	x22, elr_el1
 	mrs	x23, spsr_el1
+#ifdef CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK
+	/*
+	 * FEAT_MTE and FEAT_BTI are absent from Harmony's guest identity.  Do not
+	 * retain physical exception-entry TCO/BTYPE residue in struct pt_regs.
+	 */
+	bic	x23, x23, #PSR_TCO_BIT
+	bic	x23, x23, #PSR_BTYPE_MASK
+#endif
 	stp	lr, x21, [sp, #S_LR]
 
 	/*
